the Manu National Park is located in  southeastern Peru in the Amazon basin  much of the park is rainforest Rob  Williams of the Zoological Society of  Frankfurt Germany wants to help preserve  this ecosystem he heads a project that  is partially funded by the German  Environment ministries international  climate initiative a dozen species of  reptiles live here Caymans can grow up  to six meters in length countless  species of mammals and birds also live  in menu the extensive dense forest has  not been fully explored it no doubt  still holds many secrets people also  live in the National Park indigenous  groups such as the matcha Ginga Rob  Williams and his team visit them  regularly today they're talking to the  children about how important it is to  take care of the world they live in the  Amazon ecosystem the challenge for these  communities and we're just really trying  to help them out find their way in this  is to see what they can do so they can  maintain the traditional way of living  for those who want to live that way and  those who want to earn money live in the  monetary economy outside the park can  also have a foot in that world and and  that's the challenge for each individual  and for the community as a whole many  families have cleared small plots in the  forest to grow crops such as cassava  most of the matcha gangga are hunters  fishermen and gatherers  fish and forest animals are important  sources of protein only the men go  hunting with bow and arrow like their  fathers and grandfathers before them it  would be easier with guns but the park  administration persuaded the hunters to  do without firearms so they wouldn't  kill too many animals too quickly today  these men find and kill a woolly monkey  the machi kanga and the park  administration have made a deal  they help preserve the ecosystem in  return for certain benefits the hunters  keep a list of the animals they beg  basically the idea is that communities  can really have information about how  much they're hunting and what the state  of the populations are out in the areas  they're hunting are and then hopefully  they can think about taking some  decisions towards management of that  resource to ensure that it's there  forever the much again go demonstrate  that it is possible for humans to  actively contribute to the maintenance  of their ecosystem and its biodiversity
 

